## 5. Required Interface Reachability Contract

This block states the required interface identity, resolution, invocation, input shape, and setup that the implementation must realize. It does not state expected outcomes, prescribe private implementation structure, or expand edit scope.

```text
test_interface = cli (signals: ['"'"'cli-executable-path'"'"', '"'"'extension-name-option'"'"', '"'"'file-argument'"'"', '"'"'stdin-input'"'"', '"'"'subprocess-harness'"'"'])

This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  A native executable invoked by absolute path; every caller spawns it as a
  subprocess with the table extension enabled, using the same two shapes:
      subprocess.run(["/app/build/src/cmark-gfm", "--extension", "table", path], ...)
      subprocess.run(["/app/build/src/cmark-gfm", "--extension", "table"], input=document_text, ...)
  The binary is the program target built from the cmark-gfm C source tree
  staged at /app by the image build; both calling suites reach it only
  through this executable, never through the sources or a library import.
  It is produced by the project'"'"'s own CMake build run from /app:
      mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && make -j$(nproc)
  which places the executable at /app/build/src/cmark-gfm. Runtime is
  Linux x86-64 (dynamically linked ELF); all caller paths are absolute, so
  the working directory is irrelevant.

## Must provide
  /app/build/src/cmark-gfm   executable binary at this exact absolute path;
                             one caller asserts the file exists before doing
                             anything else
  --extension NAME           CLI option (-e short form); its argument is
                             looked up by name in the syntax-extension
                             registry (cmark_find_syntax_extension) and
                             attached to the parser
                             (cmark_parser_attach_syntax_extension); callers
                             pass table
  table                      name under which the table syntax extension is
                             registered by
                             cmark_gfm_core_extensions_ensure_registered(),
                             which the program calls at startup
  FILE positional arguments  zero or more input file paths; when none are
                             given, the document is read from stdin

## Called as
  File form:
      /app/build/src/cmark-gfm --extension table <path>
    --extension   option; the following argv element; a string, the
                  extension name (callers pass table)
    <path>        trailing positional argv element; a string path to a
                  caller-created temporary file (name ends in .md, located
                  under a temporary directory); the caller unlinks the file
                  after each run; the file holds the input document text
  Stdin form:
      /app/build/src/cmark-gfm --extension table
    no file arguments; the input document text is fed on the process stdin
    (callers pass it as a string via subprocess input=)
  Both forms are spawned through subprocess.run with capture_output=True and
  text=True, with a per-call subprocess timeout.

## Setup required
  The binary at /app/build/src/cmark-gfm must be the product of building the
  /app source tree (CMake out-of-tree build in /app/build). Callers exec the
  binary directly by absolute path and nothing on the calling side rebuilds
  it, so any change to the sources must be followed by a rebuild that
  replaces that exact path.
  The core-extensions registration that runs at program startup must keep
  registering the table syntax extension under the name table, so the name
  resolves when --extension table is parsed on the command line.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest-driven runner that spawns the binary through
  subprocess, writes input documents into temporary files it creates and
  deletes itself, feeds text to stdin itself, and measures elapsed time
  itself. A shell entrypoint installs pytest when absent and then launches
  the suites in one session. All of this belongs to the caller, not to the
  implementation; the engineer does not rebuild any of it. Files staged
  under /task-deps by the image build are not referenced by any caller and
  are not part of the interface.
