## Test harness boundary
  The following belong to the calling side, not to the implementation:
  - A pytest runner (installed via uv) that writes each temporary C source
    file, compiles it with /usr/bin/gcc to a binary under /tmp, runs that
    binary via subprocess, and inspects its captured stdout and stderr
    (including a final sentinel line the runner looks for in stdout).
  - The embedded C program text itself — tree construction, set calls, the
    unite/union call, get calls, and printf reporting are generated by the
    callers from inline templates. The implementation is the library those
    programs link against, not the programs.
  - A second caller that compiles the same kind of harness and executes the
    binary under /usr/bin/valgrind with --error-exitcode=99
    --leak-check=no --errors-for-leak-kinds=none, inspecting both output
    streams of that process.
  - The /usr/bin/ldd and /usr/bin/nm invocations used to check where the
    harness binary resolves libcomps and which symbols it references.
  - A small libc-only program that one caller compiles and runs under the same
    valgrind invocation to prove the instrumentation is live; it references
    nothing from the project.
  The engineer does not rebuild gcc, ldd, nm, valgrind, the pytest
  environment, or any of the embedded programs.
