test_interface = socket/service (signals: ['"'"'cli'"'"', '"'"'compiled-binary'"'"', '"'"'config-file'"'"', '"'"'socket'"'"', '"'"'subprocess-harness'"'"'])

This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  The target is the booth daemon binary, a native ELF program at a fixed path,
  built from the booth source tree rooted at /app:
      /app/src/boothd
  It is project-owned: produced by building the booth sources in /app
  (autogen.sh && ./configure && make), not a preinstalled system tool or test
  helper. Callers launch it as a daemon, then reach it by opening a TCP
  connection to the port named in the config and exchanging booth protocol
  messages:
      ["/app/src/boothd", "arbitrator", "-D", "-c", conf_path]
      socket.create_connection(("127.0.0.1", port), timeout=...)
  The same binary is also reached as a CLI client:
      ["/app/src/boothd", "list", "-c", conf_path]
  Runtime/working context: the build runs in /app; the daemon reads the config
  path supplied with -c and listens on the TCP port that config declares.

## Must provide
  /app/src/boothd            compiled ELF executable built from the booth source
                             in /app; a real native program, not a script or stub
  boothd arbitrator          daemon-role entrypoint; accepts -D and -c <conf>
  boothd list                client entrypoint; accepts -c <conf>
  boothd --help / --version  usage and version entrypoints
  -c <path>                  selects the booth config file for daemon and client
  TCP service                a listener on the config'"'"'s port that accepts booth
                             protocol request messages and sends booth protocol
                             replies

## Called as
  Daemon launch (subprocess argv):
      ["/app/src/boothd", "arbitrator", "-D", "-c", conf_path]
        argv[1]  "arbitrator" — role subcommand; a string
        argv[2]  "-D"         — run-in-foreground flag; a string
        argv[3]  "-c"         — config option; a string
        argv[4]  conf_path    — path to a booth config file; a string

  Network request (one TCP exchange against the daemon'"'"'s port):
      tcp_exchange(message, port, host="127.0.0.1", timeout=...)
        message — 1st positional; a bytes object holding one serialized booth
                  protocol list-request message (header plus trailing structure;
                  see Setup required)
        port    — 2nd positional; a TCP port number taken from the config
        host    — "127.0.0.1"
  The caller writes raw bytes to the socket and reads raw bytes back.

  Client list (subprocess argv):
      ["/app/src/boothd", "list", "-c", conf_path]
        argv[1]  "list"     — client subcommand; a string
        argv[2]  "-c"       — config option; a string
        argv[3]  conf_path  — path to a booth config file; a string

  Config file passed via -c (caller-authored, INI-like "key=\"value\"" lines):
      transport="<string>"   a transport name
      port="<number>"        the TCP port for the daemon
      authfile="<path>"      a path to a key file
      arbitrator="<host>"    a host address
      site="<host>"          a host address
      ticket="<name>"        a ticket name
          expire = <number>  a per-ticket option nested under the ticket

## Setup required
  - The binary must already be built and present at /app/src/boothd as a native
    ELF: the booth source in /app is built with autogen.sh, ./configure and
    make, against the glib, libxml2, libgcrypt, cluster-glue and pacemaker
    development libraries. These build/runtime libraries are preexisting system
    prerequisites, not part of the implementation.
  - The caller itself spawns the daemon with -c <conf> and connects to the port
    that <conf> declares; the daemon must come up listening on that TCP port so
    a caller can connect to it.
  - Wire framing (booth protocol over TCP), network byte order throughout: each
    list request is a fixed-length message made of a 48-byte header — twelve
    big-endian uint32 fields in the order opts, secs, usecs, magic, version,
    from, length, cmd, request, options, reason, result — followed by a 28-byte
    trailing structure: one big-endian uint32 hash identifier and a 24-byte hash
    field. Protocol constants: magic 0x5F1BA08C, version 0x00010003, and the
    list command is the four ASCII bytes '"'"'C'"'"','"'"'L'"'"','"'"'s'"'"','"'"'t'"'"' packed as one
    big-endian uint32. The length field carries the total message length in
    bytes.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side supplies everything that reaches the binary and is not part
  of the implementation: a Python harness that writes a fresh key file and a
  booth config into a private temporary directory, spawns the daemon through
  subprocess, waits for its TCP port, opens a socket, serializes and sends the
  request bytes, and reads the reply bytes; and a pytest runner that invokes the
  binary through subprocess for the usage/version and client-list entrypoints
  and inspects the file on disk. None of this is rebuilt by the implementation;
  the engineer provides only the booth program at /app/src/boothd.
```

