```
- **Required interface:**
  ```c
  GF_Err mpgviddmx_process(GF_Filter *filter);
  ```
  Wired as `.process` of the `rfmpgvid` filter register by retained code later in this file; the filter core calls it repeatedly until `GF_EOS`. It calls the file-local helpers retained outside the region, which provide the duration/index scan, output-PID configuration on parsed headers, queued-packet send/flush, next-start-code search, and timestamp advance.
- **Observable behavior:** At most one input packet is reframed per call; the retained duration/index scan runs while the output duration is unknown. No packet available: if the input is EOS, flush queued packets, mark the output PID EOS, release the held source-packet reference, and return `GF_EOS`; otherwise return `GF_OK`. With an adopted input timescale: CTS/DTS adopt the packet'"'"'s timestamps (once only when CTS recomputation is enabled), the per-frame duration becomes the smallest DTS difference observed between packets, and emitted packets merge this packet'"'"'s properties. Header data incomplete within one packet is stored and aggregated with following packets; while data remains insufficient, the input packet is dropped and `GF_OK` is returned. A parsed MPEG-1/2 sequence/extension header or MPEG-4 VOL header configures the output PID; MPEG-4 also reads the profile/level byte following the VOS start code and exports the byte span from the VOS start to the end of the parsed VOL as decoder configuration; other start codes within that span are skipped. Byte spans preceding a start code are forwarded as packets without timestamps. Each parsed frame is emitted as a packet starting at its start code with the current CTS/DTS, the per-frame duration, start framing, end framing only when the frame is complete or the input packet is an access-unit end, SAP_1 for I-frames only, the seek flag while a seek is being consumed, and a carousel marker consumed by the retained flush logic to rework queued-packet timestamps — withheld when the input packet signals access-unit start. B-frame packets are queued and flushed before the next reference frame'"'"'s packet. Non-coded frames are skipped under `vfr` or packed-B handling (time still advances) and emitted otherwise. Frames whose data spans packet boundaries are emitted once complete; trailing bytes that may begin a start code carry into the next packet.
- **Inputs:** The filter; packets of the single configured input PID (MPEG-1/2/4 elementary stream bytes); context state: arguments, playing/seek flags, stored and carried bytes, current timestamps.
- **Outputs and errors:** `GF_OK` on the paths described above, and `GF_EOS` after the end-of-stream flush; output packets sent on the output PID; context timestamps, frame counters, and carry state updated.
- **Side effects and interactions:** Consumed input packets are dropped; queued packets are sent through the retained dispatch helper; the output PID is created/configured through the retained helper; the parse position is remembered and later resumed while playback has not started or the output would block.
- **Required sequencing and state:** The EOS flush precedes marking the output PID EOS; nothing is emitted before the output PID exists (data before the first successful header parse is skipped); queued packets are flushed before each reference frame; timestamps advance one frame duration per emitted or `vfr`-skipped frame.
- **Region boundary:** Self-contained — the range spans the whole function definition; retained text above ends a retained helper and retained text below begins the next function, so nothing is left open. Mechanical consequence of the cut: the retained register struct further down references `mpgviddmx_process`, so that exact name and signature must be in scope when the region is restored.
