## Reached at
  An absolute-path executable that the callers launch as a subprocess through a
  shared driver:
      cmd = ["/usr/local/bin/gpac", "-noprog", "-i", input_path, "rfmpgvid", "inspect"]
  and, for a separate instrumentation-liveness run:
      ["/usr/local/bin/gpac", "-version"]
  /usr/local/bin/gpac is the project-owned build artifact: it is produced by
  building the GPAC source tree staged by the image build at /app/gpac and
  installing it. It is not a system package and nothing else on PATH satisfies
  the callers; the callers fail outright if the file is missing or not
  executable. The chain members rfmpgvid and inspect are filter names resolved
  from the filters compiled into this installed program and its libraries. The
  callers use absolute paths and do not depend on the working directory.

## Must provide
  /usr/local/bin/gpac   executable file; AddressSanitizer-instrumented build
                        artifact of the project source tree
  -i <file>             source option taking a media-file path
  -noprog               option flag taking no value
  -version              option taking no further arguments
  rfmpgvid              the MPEG video reframer filter, loadable by this name
                        in a filter chain
  inspect               an output (sink) filter, loadable by this name at the
                        end of a filter chain

## Called as
  gpac -noprog -i <input> rfmpgvid inspect
      -i <input>   the option value; a string filesystem path to a regular
                   file. The callers write the file bytes themselves before
                   the call (raw bytes forming MPEG video elementary streams,
                   more than one such file per run); one call passes a fixed
                   pre-existing media file that ships with the calling side
                   and whose integrity the callers check before use.
      rfmpgvid     positional chain argument; a filter name
      inspect      positional chain argument; a filter name, last in the chain
      -noprog      flag, no value
  gpac -version
      no arguments; the callers run it with the environment variable
      ASAN_OPTIONS set to raise sanitizer verbosity

## Setup required
  - The source tree behind the artifact is the GPAC checkout at /app/gpac
    (staged by the image build; no version-control metadata retained). Filter
    code reaches the callers only through a rebuild and reinstall of that
    tree: ./configure --enable-sanitizer, make, make install, ldconfig — the
    same sequence the image build used. The installed binary must remain
    AddressSanitizer-instrumented: one caller run executes -version under a
    verbosity-raising ASAN_OPTIONS setting and depends on evidence of the
    instrumentation, so a plain uninstrumented rebuild or a wrapper in place
    of the real binary does not satisfy the callers.
  - The callers pin their own process environment for every run (they set
    ASAN_OPTIONS and remove LD_PRELOAD); the implementation supplies no
    environment and no service.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side, none of which is part of the implementation:
  - a pytest runner installed into a dedicated virtualenv at /opt/vtest by a
    setup script, which executes the caller suite inside the container;
  - a driver module that builds the subprocess command above, pins the
    sanitizer-runtime environment, captures combined stdout/stderr, and
    interprets process termination and output markers;
  - a generator module that builds the input byte streams and writes them to
    temporary files (the callers generate their own media and do not reuse
    media shipped in the image);
  - a small separate C program compiled fresh with gcc -fsanitize=address on
    every run and executed to confirm the sanitizer machinery in this
    environment is live; this canary, its compilation, and its run belong to
    the callers;
  - the pre-existing media file passed in one call, together with its
    integrity verification, which ships with the calling side.
  The engineer does not rebuild or modify any of these.
