## Reached at
  The OpenStack Keystone package staged at /app by the image build (upstream
  keystone at commit 4bfa203ac433da1537d8da963bd7554d36f2add7), reached
  in-process through its own WSGI pipeline — not over a socket. A driver pins
  the app tree to the front of sys.path and loads the admin composite with Paste
  Deploy:

      sys.path.insert(0, '"'"'/app'"'"')
      import keystone
      from paste.deploy import loadapp
      app = loadapp('"'"'config:/tests/ks_test.conf'"'"', name='"'"'admin'"'"')

  The Paste composite [composite:admin] maps /v2.0 to [pipeline:admin_api]:

      url_normalize token_auth admin_token_auth xml_body json_body debug \
      ec2_extension crud_extension admin_service

  whose terminal app is keystone.service:admin_app_factory. The loaded pipeline
  is invoked as a WSGI callable:

      app(environ, start_response)

  Resolution is guarded: keystone, keystone.catalog.core, keystone.identity.core,
  and keystone.service must each resolve (realpath) under /app, or the run
  aborts rather than producing an observation. Runtime: Python 2.7 at
  /usr/local/bin/python; working directory /app.

## Must provide
  keystone                                            importable package at /app
  keystone.service                                    module (imported by the driver)
  keystone.service:admin_app_factory                  paste app_factory for [app:admin_service]
  keystone.catalog.core                               module owning the OS-KSADM service routes
  keystone.identity.core                              module owning the tenant/user/role routes
  keystone.middleware:NormalizingFilter.factory       [filter:url_normalize]
  keystone.middleware:TokenAuthMiddleware.factory     [filter:token_auth]
  keystone.middleware:AdminTokenAuthMiddleware.factory [filter:admin_token_auth]
  keystone.middleware:XmlBodyMiddleware.factory      [filter:xml_body]
  keystone.middleware:JsonBodyMiddleware.factory      [filter:json_body]
  keystone.common.wsgi:Debug.factory                  [filter:debug]
  keystone.contrib.admin_crud:CrudExtension.factory  [filter:crud_extension]
  keystone.contrib.ec2:Ec2Extension.factory           [filter:ec2_extension]

  Routes wired under /v2.0 on the admin_service app (the paths callers drive):
  POST   /v2.0/OS-KSADM/services
  GET    /v2.0/OS-KSADM/services
  GET    /v2.0/OS-KSADM/services/{id}
  DELETE /v2.0/OS-KSADM/services/{id}
  POST   /v2.0/tenants
  POST   /v2.0/users
  POST   /v2.0/OS-KSADM/roles
  PUT    /v2.0/tenants/{tenant_id}/users/{user_id}/roles/OS-KSADM/{role_id}
  GET    /v2.0/tenants/{tenant_id}/users/{user_id}/roles
  GET    /v2.0/users/{user_id}/roles

## Called as
  Callers reach the implementation through one transport function, which the
  harness translates into a single in-process WSGI invocation:

      ks_client.drive(method, path, token=None, body=None)
        method — 1st positional; a string (HTTP method)
        path   — 2nd positional; a string beginning with /v2.0/...
        token  — keyword; a string, or None (omitted), mapped to the
                 X-Auth-Token request header when present and to its absence
                 when None
        body   — keyword; a dict (JSON-serialized into the request body), or None

  The harness builds the WSGI environ and calls the loaded app directly:

      app(environ, start_response)
        environ['"'"'REQUEST_METHOD'"'"']   — the method string
        environ['"'"'PATH_INFO'"'"']        — the path string
        environ['"'"'HTTP_X_AUTH_TOKEN'"'"']— present only when token is not None
        environ['"'"'wsgi.input'"'"']       — a stream over the JSON body string
        environ['"'"'CONTENT_TYPE'"'"']     — '"'"'application/json'"'"' when a body is given
        environ['"'"'CONTENT_LENGTH'"'"']  — the byte length of the body string when given
        environ['"'"'HTTP_ACCEPT'"'"']      — '"'"'application/json'"'"'

## Setup required
  Environment and working directory:
    The interpreter is Python 2.7 at /usr/local/bin/python, launched with
    working directory /app. PYTHONPATH, LD_PRELOAD, and LD_LIBRARY_PATH are
    scrubbed from the environment before launch, and /app is pinned to the front
    of sys.path. KS_DRIVER_CONF must name the Paste config (/tests/ks_test.conf).

  Paste configuration (the file at KS_DRIVER_CONF):
    Must define [composite:admin] mapping /v2.0 to [pipeline:admin_api], with
    [app:admin_service] = keystone.service:admin_app_factory as the terminal app
    and every filter factory listed above resolvable. admin_token in [DEFAULT]
    is the shared secret carried as the X-Auth-Token value by authenticated
    calls.

  Backends and state:
    [identity] driver keystone.identity.backends.sql.Identity,
    [catalog] driver keystone.catalog.backends.sql.Catalog, and
    [token] driver keystone.token.backends.kvs.Token, backed by the SQLite
    database at /var/lib/keystone/keystone.db, which must be initialized
    (bin/keystone-manage db_sync) before the API can create or return records.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side — not part of the implementation — is a pytest runner that
  invokes the application'"'"'s own interpreter through `subprocess.run`, running an
  in-process WSGI driver. That driver loads the admin pipeline via Paste Deploy,
  constructs a WSGI environ from the method/path/token/body the runner passes,
  calls the loaded app directly (no network hop, so nothing listening on the
  service ports can intercept or remap the observation), and returns the WSGI
  response to the runner as a parsed object. A separate integrity check imports
  keystone, keystone.catalog.core, and keystone.identity.core and asserts each
  resolved under /app. The driver script, the transport module that spawns it,
  the Paste config it reads, and the runner that waits on the service port before
  invoking the suite are all caller-owned; the engineer does not rebuild them.
