Masked section 2:
```
- **Required interface:** Exact signature consumed within this file:
```c
static void push_bracket(subject *subj, bool image, cmark_node *inl_text);
```
File-local (`static`); not declared in any header. Reached by the retained inline parse loop in `parse_inline` (this file): on encountering `[` it advances past the `[`, emits a literal-text node, and calls `push_bracket(subj, false, that_node)` (line 1401); on `![` followed by `[` (and not `[^`) it does the same with `push_bracket(subj, true, that_node)` (line 1411). The sibling retained function `pop_bracket` (line 490) is the inverse that removes from the same stack. `subject` is the file-local struct whose `last_bracket` member this function updates; `bracket` is the file-local type from r0.
- **Observable behavior:** Pushes one opener record onto the bracket stack. Before pushing, if a bracket already exists on the stack, that prior bracket is marked as having a bracket after it (`bracket_after = true`). The new record is initialized so that, among its state, `active = true`, `image` is set from the argument, `inl_text` set to the supplied opener text node, `previous` linked to the current stack top, `previous_delimiter` set to the current delimiter-stack tail (`subj->last_delim`), `position` set to the current byte offset (`subj->pos`), and `bracket_after = false`. The stack top (`subj->last_bracket`) becomes the new record.
- **Inputs:** `subj` — the parser state whose `mem` (a `cmark_mem *` allocator), `last_bracket`, `last_delim`, and `pos` are read and whose `last_bracket` is written; `image` — nonzero for an image (`![`) opener, zero for a link (`[`) opener; `inl_text` — the inline text node holding the literal `[` or `![`.
