## 5. Required Interface Reachability Contract

This block states the required interface identity, resolution, invocation, input shape, and setup that the implementation must realize. It does not state expected outcomes, prescribe private implementation structure, or expand edit scope.

```text
test_interface = cli (signals: ['"'"'build-artifact-path'"'"', '"'"'cli-extension-flag'"'"', '"'"'elf-binary-target'"'"', '"'"'stdin-stdout-transport'"'"', '"'"'subprocess-exec'"'"'])

This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  A native ELF executable built from the cmark-gfm project source and installed
  by the image build at the absolute path:
      /app/build/src/cmark-gfm
  The image build clones the project (pinned tag 0.29.0.gfm.5) into /app, then
  builds it with:
      mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release && make -j$(nproc)
  producing the binary at /app/build/src/cmark-gfm. /app/build/src is also
  prepended to PATH. Callers invoke it by absolute path with no working-
  directory dependence.

## Must provide
  /app/build/src/cmark-gfm              a native ELF executable (the produced build artifact)
  -e <extension>                        CLI option selecting a registered built-in extension by name
  -e autolink                           the autolink extension, selectable via the option above
  --version                              standard version flag (used by the image build to verify the binary)
  stdin acceptance                       the executable reads its input document from stdin
  stdout output                          the executable writes its rendered output to stdout

## Called as
  subprocess.run([CMARK_BIN], input=<text>, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=<num>)
      CMARK_BIN  —  argv[0], the absolute path "/app/build/src/cmark-gfm"
      input      —  keyword argument; a string (text mode), the document text
      timeout    —  keyword argument; a number

  subprocess.run([CMARK_BIN, "-e", "autolink"], input=<text>, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=<num>)
      CMARK_BIN     —  argv[0], the absolute path "/app/build/src/cmark-gfm"
      "-e"          —  argv[1], the extension-selection flag, a literal token
      "autolink"    —  argv[2], the extension name, a literal token
      input         —  keyword argument; a string (text mode), the document text
      timeout       —  keyword argument; a number

  Both argv shapes are used: the bare binary, and the binary with the autolink
  extension enabled. The extension-enabled shape is the route both call sites
  travel through.

## Setup required
  The image build must have completed: the cmark-gfm project source present
  under /app and compiled via cmake + make into /app/build, yielding
  /app/build/src/cmark-gfm as a native ELF executable (callers reject any non-
  ELF file at that path, so a wrapper script cannot stand in for the binary).
  The autolink extension must be available as a registered extension
  selectable through the -e flag. No service startup, port, or network
  readiness is involved.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest runner (launched by a shell script that runs
  python3 -m pytest) which invokes the binary through subprocess.run, passing
  the document on stdin and capturing stdout in text mode. A helper in the
  calling side wraps subprocess.run to add the autolink flag and a timeout, and
  the calling side also reads the first bytes of the binary to assert it is a
  native ELF file. These belong to the caller, not to the implementation; the
  engineer reconstructs the compiled binary and its extensions, not the runner,
  the subprocess transport, the helper, or the ELF check.
```

