## Reached at
  A Django HTTP service; callers speak plain HTTP GET to it over the network.
  The base URL and route the callers build:

      BASE_URL = os.environ.get("GEONODE_URL", "http://localhost:8000").rstrip("/")
      PROXY_ENDPOINT = f"{BASE_URL}/proxy/"
      ...
      query = urllib.parse.urlencode({"url": target_url})
      http_get(f"{PROXY_ENDPOINT}?{query}", timeout=timeout)

  The route is owned by the project application staged at /app and registered in
  its URLconf. The view module and its registration, as written:

      from geonode.proxy.views import proxy          # /app/geonode/proxy/views.py
      urlpatterns = [
          path('"'"'proxy/'"'"', proxy, name='"'"'proxy'"'"'),       # /app/urls.py, ROOT_URLCONF = '"'"'urls'"'"'
      ]

  Runtime and working directory for the service (the callers do not depend on a
  working directory; the server startup does):

      cd /app && python manage.py runserver 0.0.0.0:8000     # DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=settings

## Must provide
  proxy                      Django view callable in module geonode.proxy.views;
                             accepts a single Django request object
  GET /proxy/                URL route mapped to the proxy view (name '"'"'proxy'"'"');
                             reachable with and without a url query parameter
  url                        GET query parameter read from the request by the route
  settings                   importable Django settings module (DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE),
                             exposing SITEURL, PROXY_ALLOWED_HOSTS, DEBUG, SECRET_KEY
  ROOT_URLCONF = '"'"'urls'"'"'      URLconf module that registers the proxy/ route
  :8000                      the service listening endpoint callers target

## Called as
  GET {BASE_URL}/proxy/                no url parameter present; the route must still
                                       accept the request
  GET {BASE_URL}/proxy/?url=<target>   url — GET query parameter; a string. Callers pass
                                       a path string beginning with "/", an absolute URL
                                       string (which may itself carry a query string), or
                                       another URL-like string
  GET {BASE_URL}/<path>                direct fetch used as the live-route control; <path>
                                       is a freshly generated arbitrary path string
  GET {BASE_URL}/proxy/?url=/<path>    the same path sent through the proxy route as the
                                       url parameter; paired with the direct fetch above

## Setup required
  - The service must be listening on 0.0.0.0:8000, started from working directory
    /app with `python manage.py runserver 0.0.0.0:8000` and
    DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=settings; it is restarted in place while running.
  - Environment for the service: SITEURL, SECRET_KEY, and DEBUG must be set; the
    settings module derives the allowed-proxy host list (PROXY_ALLOWED_HOSTS) from
    the SITEURL hostname. The view reads settings.PROXY_ALLOWED_HOSTS,
    settings.SITEURL, and settings.DEBUG.
  - Callers resolve the base URL from the GEONODE_URL environment variable,
    defaulting to http://localhost:8000.
  - Before asserting anything, callers poll GET {BASE_URL}/proxy/ until a non-5xx
    response is observed; the route must answer this probe.
  - Django and requests are installed in the service environment (the call to fetch
    the proxied target runs server-side).

## Test harness boundary
  A pytest runner reaches the service purely as an HTTP client: it builds a standard
  library urllib opener that ignores any ambient proxy configuration and does not
  follow redirects, and issues GET requests to the URLs above. A shell runner
  installs the test framework from bundled wheels, waits for the service to answer,
  and then runs the suite; a small entrypoint restart loop keeps the Django server
  process alive. None of this — the HTTP client, the readiness poll, the wheel-based
  installer, or the restart loop — is part of the implementation, and none of it
  should be rebuilt. The implementation is only the Django application under /app
  that serves the /proxy/ route.
