This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  Two caller surfaces, both build outputs of the C/C++ source tree that the
  image build checks out at /app (AFFLIBv3 at tag v3.7.16, built in place
  with its own autotools pipeline: bootstrap.sh, ./configure, make).

  1. Executables exec'"'"'d by absolute path with a single path argument:
         subprocess.run(["/app/tools/affcat", path], capture_output=True, timeout=30)
         subprocess.run(["/app/tools/affinfo", path], capture_output=True, timeout=30)
  2. The shared library loaded by absolute path through ctypes:
         lib = ctypes.CDLL(path)
     where path is resolved by listing /app/lib/.libs, taking the first
     entry starting with libafflib.so that is not a symlink and whose
     realpath stays under /app. In this build that resolves to
     /app/lib/.libs/libafflib.so.0.0.0.

  Callers never use PATH (the image prepends /app/tools to PATH, but every
  reference above is absolute) and never depend on the working directory.

## Must provide
  /app/tools/affcat               executable binary produced by the /app build
  /app/tools/affinfo              executable binary produced by the /app build
  /app/lib/.libs/libafflib.so*    shared object produced by the /app build;
                                  the resolved real (non-symlink) file in
                                  this build is libafflib.so.0.0.0
  af_open                         exported C symbol; callers declare it
                                  (c_char_p, c_int, c_int) -> c_void_p
  af_read                         exported C symbol; callers declare it
                                  (c_void_p, c_char_p, c_ssize_t) -> c_ssize_t
  af_close                        exported C symbol; callers declare it
                                  (c_void_p) -> c_int
  af_get_pagesize                 exported C symbol; callers declare it
                                  (c_void_p) -> c_int

## Called as
  Both tools are invoked with the same shape:
      [tool, path]    path — 1st positional argument; a string, the
                      filesystem path of an AFF image file written by the
                      caller. One argument only; stdin/stdout/stderr are
                      captured, not shared with a terminal; each invocation
                      is bounded at 30 seconds.

  The library is invoked through the ctypes declarations above, in this
  order, once per file:
      af = lib.af_open(path.encode("utf-8"), 0, 0)
      pagesize = lib.af_get_pagesize(af)
      buf = ctypes.create_string_buffer(65536)
      n = lib.af_read(af, buf, 65536)
      lib.af_close(af)
      af_open          1st positional — a filesystem path as UTF-8-encoded
                       bytes (c_char_p); 2nd positional — an int (open
                       flags; the observed calls pass 0); 3rd positional —
                       an int (mode; the observed calls pass 0). The caller
                       branches on whether the returned handle is null.
      af_get_pagesize  1st positional — the handle from af_open (c_void_p).
      af_read          1st positional — the handle (c_void_p); 2nd
                       positional — a writable buffer allocated by the
                       caller (c_char_p, 65536 bytes); 3rd positional — a
                       signed byte count (c_ssize_t; the observed calls
                       pass 65536). The caller reads bytes back out of the
                       same buffer.
      af_close         1st positional — the handle (c_void_p).

  The file handed to both transports is authored by the caller in the AFF
  on-disk format:
      file magic  b"AFF10\r\n\x00"
      then segments; each segment is
          b"AFF\x00"                    4-byte segment magic
          uint32 name_len, uint32 data_len, uint32 arg   (big-endian)
          name bytes (UTF-8, no terminator)
          data bytes
          b"ATT\x00"                    4-byte tail magic
          uint32 total segment length = 16 + name_len + data_len + 8
      The caller writes three segments per file: pagesize (an integer
      carried in the arg field, empty data), sectorsize (an integer
      carried in the arg field, empty data), and page0 (flags carried in
      the arg field, page content bytes carried in the data). The
      integers the caller writes into these arg fields are part of the
      file'"'"'s metadata and are chosen per file.

## Setup required
  - The build of the /app tree must have produced both executables and the
    shared object: callers check that each tool is a regular executable
    file at its absolute path before exec'"'"'ing it, and they resolve the
    library only inside /app/lib/.libs.
  - Library ownership pinning: the loaded file must not be a symlink and
    its realpath must stay under /app. A copy installed on a system path
    or staged anywhere else is never loaded.
  - Runtime: Linux container (ubuntu:20.04-based image), working directory
    set to /app by the image but not relied on by callers. No environment
    variables, no service startup, and no registration beyond the build
    outputs themselves are required.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side — none of which is part of the implementation, and none
  of which should be rebuilt — consists of:
  - a pytest runner installed into a dedicated venv at /opt/pytest-venv,
    which runs the suite with absolute paths;
  - a caller-side helper module that writes the AFF input files into
    temporary paths, execs the tools through subprocess, loads the library
    with ctypes, and runs each library interaction inside a forked child
    process whose outcome is reported back over a pipe;
  - a Python input-generation script staged at the root of /app by the
    image build; it exists to write AFF image files for callers to hand to
    the tools and is not part of the library or the tools.
